{"id": "q0218", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3208304725, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "923cbbdfe3213927dfa36b1f8e0eb48a30d641d9b9bf7cf25d16765340d88836", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 4, "query": "Which of the following is not correct for the perfectly competitive firm, in the long run?\n\nA. price = marginal revenue.\nB. price = minimum average cost.\nC. price = minimum average variable cost.\nD. price = marginal cost.\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0218.wav", "input_sha256": "808655640e3b5a30df6a8bd7fc962ff3c6835ccaf2b314684b6a92eba795735f", "input_samples": 267400, "input_s": 16.712, "n_real_chunks": 17, "score": 0.4494, "act": 0.9994, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "The answer is C. Price equals minimum average variable cost is not correct for a perfectly competitive firm in the long run. In perfect competition, firms earn zero economic profit in the long run because price equals minimum average total cost, which includes both fixed and variable costs. Therefore, the condition that price equals minimum average variable cost does not hold true as it ignores fixed costs necessary for covering all costs in the long run equilibrium.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 16, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 17, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0218_local_local.wav", "sha256": "5fb75cbff8675326ad165b5d71e8ff7f9eadabbcf68230e6bc33b90ad7c758d5", "sr": 24000, "secon